      "title": "Roth describes a different tax treatment",
      "narration": "Elena considers a Roth individual retirement arrangement. Regular Roth contributions are made with after-tax money and are not deductible. That is different from a traditional contribution that qualifies for a current deduction. Roth eligibility still requires the applicable compensation and contribution rules, and direct regular contributions can be reduced or prohibited by modified adjusted gross income and filing status. A high income that prevents a direct Roth contribution is not the same rule as the traditional deduction phaseout. Nor does it automatically answer whether a conversion is possible. Separate regular contributions, transfers, conversions and distributions before applying a limit. Those words describe different transactions.",
